      "output": {
        "decision": "answer",
        "reason": "The evidence fully addresses the question. The abstract (c-204aa49a...) states that Twitter data makes better predictions about the language inventory in each country. The Discussion section (c-9887004c...) offers hypotheses relevant to why Twitter's representation differs: Twitter data matches populations better when weighted by GDP and worse when weighted by internet-usage statistics, implying Twitter represents more wealthy populations than web-crawled data, and alternatively that the Twitter collection is urban-centered (urban areas tend to be wealthier). The Population Demographics section (c-01b9a363...) further notes Twitter provides better representation for North America and Oceania and explains that Twitter has fewer missing languages (fewer false negatives), which underlies the better prediction of language inventory. The authors also note English and Spanish are very common on Twitter, giving countries using these languages good representation by default, and they explicitly state more research is needed to distinguish immigration, tourism, and business as sources of false positives. Thus hypotheses are offered."
      },
      "elapsed_ms": 3398.950499948114
